TI  - Tri-modal adhesion switchable hydrogel with precise regulation of interfacial states via a solvent microenvironment reconstruction strategy
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Switchable adhesive hydrogel
KW  - Solvent-triggered
KW  - Dynamic regulation
KW  - Sticky-slippery
KW  - Environmental tolerance
KW  - TISSUE ADHESIVE
AB  - As a novel and promising smart material, adhesive switchable hydrogels exhibit significant potential for applications in fields such as bioelectronics and soft robotics through dynamic regulation of their adhesive properties. However, practical applications still confront challenges including inadequate adhesive strength, slow response speeds, and poor environmental adaptability. This study addresses these issues by developing a solvent-triggered reversible adhesive hydrogel (P(AA-co-TFEA)/FCS) through a "solvent microenvironment reconstruction" strategy. The cationic surfactant (FCS) dynamically encapsulates hydrophobic trifluoroethyl acrylate (TFEA) segments while being anchored to hydrophilic polyacrylic acid (AA) chains through electrostatic interactions. The unique molecular encapsulation-anchoring mechanism endows the hydrogel with solvent-responsive adhesive switchable properties: in the dry state on the hydrogel surface, hydrophobic micelles shield the carboxyl adhesive groups, reducing surface energy (contact angle >114 degrees) and adhesive strength (<10.44 kPa), thereby facilitating storage and handling. Upon exposure to hydrophilic solvents (such as water/ethanol), the micelles in the hydrogel dissociate while exposing adhesion groups that form interactions with the interface. This results in a remarkable increase in adhesive strength >166.44 kPa (on glass substrates) within several seconds, achieving the transition of the hydrogel from a "non-stick" state to a "sticky" state. Precise control of water volume enables the transition between the adhesive and lubricious states. Under the condition of excessive water, interfacial adhesion force was disrupted through lubrication effect, achieving a "sticky-to-slippery transition". The hydrogel demonstrates exceptional reversibility, self-healing ability, and environmental tolerance (resistance to acids, bases, salts, and alcohols). In practical applications, it can serve as a solvent-sensitive adhesion switch for non-invasive organ transplantation, utilize as a sweat-adaptive wearable sensor for stable electrocardiographic signal monitoring and aid climbing robots in moving on vertical surfaces through dynamic regulation of adhesion force (F-A) and friction force (F-F). This study offers a novel perspective for the design of smart adhesive materials in fields such as flexible electronics, biomedicine, and robotics.
