KW  - Tactile sensors
KW  - Ionic liquids
KW  - Hemispherical dielectric layer
KW  - Pressure and temperature sensors
AB  - Ionic liquid-based tactile sensors offer significant advantages, such as excellent sensitivity, strong anti- interference features, low detection noise, and a comprehensive response to both dynamic and static forces. Developing a reliable bimodal tactile sensor that can detect both pressure and temperature using ionic liquids remains a significant challenge. An electrical double-layer ion sensor is formed by combining an indium tin oxide (ITO) conductive film as an electrode layer with a hemispherical dielectric layer composed of 1-Ethyl-3-methylimidazolium trifluoromethanesulfonate (EMIM][OTF]) and polyvinyl alcohol (PVA). The sensor can detect both momentary forces and relatively slow heat conduction, which allows for distinguishing synchronized stimulation by pressure and temperature response time. The pressure sensor, constructed with a dual-layer structure, demonstrates a sensitivity of 0.038 kPa- 1 and a broad detection range from 0 to 30 kPa. Moreover, it exhibits excellent repeatability (2000 cycles) and reliability, along with a rapid response time of 60 ms. Furthermore, the temperature-dependent nature of the dielectric constant of the ionic liquids allows the fabricated sensor to achieve sensitive temperature detection, exhibiting sensitivity (0.045 degrees C-1) and a detection range of 25-50 degrees C. This sensor possesses exceptional sensing capabilities, enabling it to accurately measure the pressure exerted by small objects and monitor real-time temperature fluctuations in hot water. Additionally, the sensor array has the ability to detect the proximity of fingers. This study presents a novel method for the fabrication of tactile sensors intended for wearable applications.
