AB  - Adulteration of urine samples poses a significant challenge in drug testing programs, as it is conducted to prevent the detection of illegal drugs within urine matrices and to evade medical or legal consequences. One of the in vitro urine sample chemical adulteration methods commonly employed in cases of tetrahydrocannabinol and benzodiazepine addiction involves the direct addition of Visine (R), a brand of over-the-counter eye drops that contains benzalkonium chloride (BAC) as a preservative, with the aim of reducing their detectability by immunoassay screening tests. In this work, a rapid and simple dual ratio colorimetric and smartphone-based analytical approach was developed for the detection of BAC in adulterated urine samples, confirming its presence using bromothymol blue (BTB). A colorimetric probe is devised based on the color change of BTB from blue to green at a pH of 7.5 in the presence of BAC, which was determined ratiometrically at 618 nm and 460 nm. The developed platform showed good linearity (R2 = 0.994) with LOD and LOQ of 8.91 and 27.00 mu g/mL, respectively. This color change was detected using a smartphone as a shift in color hue (H) from shades of blue (190) to green (160), thereby confirming the presence of BAC. The developed platform was successfully applied to the detection of real urine samples manipulated with the addition of Visine (R), where the developed colors were not affected by the presence of tetrahydrocannabinol or benzodiazepines. It is worth noting that the developed approach is simple, fast, green, and practical. Thus, it can be implemented in routine urine sample authenticity checking protocols in drug testing labs.
