AB  - Thermogalvanic organohydrogel (TGHs) leverage the temperature difference (Delta T) to facilitate energy supply and electrical signal sensing without requiring external batteries. The conventional preparation of thermogalvanic organohydrogel is often laborious and time-consuming, significantly hindering their widespread adoption and innovative applications. Here, a novel preparation process was developed to fabricate our TGH sensor with a rapid prototyping based on the Hofmeister effect. This process involved the combination of Fe3 + /2 + as the redox pair and poly(vinyl) alcohol (PVA) and polyethylene glycol (PEG) as the polymer network. The development enabled rapid prototyping (<6 h), room temperature prototyping (similar to 25 degrees C) and high thermoelectric potential (0.86 mV/K). Importantly, the Gel-Fe3+/2+ TGH was able to operate continuously in experiments at low temperatures(>-20 degrees C), together with excellent mechanical tensile properties. This phenomenon was found to be attributable to the strong hydrogen bonds that were formed between DMSO and H2O. Moreover, an e-skin device (smart finger cuffs) based on the Gel-Fe3+/2+ TGH technology has been demonstrated, capable of utilizing human waste heat to power wearable devices and rapidly detect the temperature (via Delta T-dependent voltage output) and volume (via multi-channel spatial voltage profiling) of water in a water cup. Our work provides a new path for a novel green and safe method for rapid synthesis of TGH sensor, thus facilitating widespread use of TGHs for temperature detection in wearable electronic devices.
