AB  - A core-sheath structured hydrogel active layer offers a promising solution to overcome the performance trade-off inherent in monolithic hydrogel-based moisture electric generators (MEG). Herein, a one-step UV-induced polymerization/gelation method is developed to fabricate such MEG devices using a poly(vinyl alcohol)/acrylic acid/phytic acid precursor. This strategy preserves the high ionic conductivity and water retention capacity of the precursor solution, while the surrounding hydrogel sheath ensures mechanical integrity and induces non-uniform water retention capacity and transportation rate. Consequently, a stable water concentration gradient is established, promoting directional ion transport and enhancing power output. With Cu/Pt electrode pairs, the optimized device achieves an open-circuit voltage of similar to 0.6 V and a volumetric current density of similar to 900 mu A/cm(3) at 55 RH% and 25 degrees C. Lastly, by taking advantage of its compact design, flexibility, and stable power generation, the device is successfully demonstrated in self-powered sensing applications for humidity, breath, and gesture monitoring. In summary, this work provides new insights into the design of high-performance ionically conductive hydrogel-enabled MEG devices and contributes to the development of sustainable green power sources, delivering a scalable and adaptive device platform that transitions from conceptual validation toward practical wearable integration.
