TI  - Carbon nanofibers-networked multi-functional ultra-fast self-healing conductive hydrogel and its sensor
T2  - MICROCHEMICAL JOURNAL
LA  - English
KW  - Flexible wearable sensor
KW  - Conductive nanocomposite organohydrogel
KW  - Multi-functional
KW  - High stretchability
KW  - Ultra-fast self-healing
KW  - TOUGH
KW  - MXENE
AB  - Conductive hydrogels (CH) hold vast potential in flexible electronics and human-computer interaction. However, integrated CH-based multifunctional sensing devices with required tensile strength, self-adhesion, selfrepair and good sensitivity/dynamic detection range still remain great challenges. In this work a conductive organohydrogel is innovatively created by addition of carbon nanofibers (CNF) into MXene and poly(3, 4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrene sulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS)-impregnated polyvinyl alcohol/polyacrylamide (PVA/ PAAM) hydrogel. Results undeniably indicate that the addition of 1-dimensional CNF prevents the hydrogel composite from aggregating while forming very uniform pore structure, largely enhancing the stretchability, greatly rising the electrocatalytic ability, and like reenforce bars to inhibit severe damages beneficial to fast selfhealing (approximate to 0.6 s). Therefore, the sensor not only excels in strain sensing but also integrates sweat detection and temperature sensing with motion for successful detection of uric acid (UA) in sweat within a range of 20 mu M to 14 mM, a limit of detection of 10.93 mu M, and real-time monitoring of temperature within a range of 16-40 degrees C. It is noted that the detection range is much broader than the UA concentration of hyperuricemia patients in sweat to meet the needs in practical clinic diagnosis and bioresearch. This study innovatively creates a CNF-enhanced MXene and PEDOT:PSS-impregnated PVA/PAAM hydrogel for sensing performance improvement, while integrating a wearable multifunctional sensing device, holing a great promise for its important applications.
