TI  - A thermo/cryo-spun reduction strategy for continue fabricating wide-range fiber-based strain sensor with high linearity and sensitivity
T2  - SENSORS AND ACTUATORS A-PHYSICAL
LA  - English
KW  - Thermo/cryo-spun reduction strategy
KW  - Hybrid sensing networks
KW  - High linear sensitivity
KW  - Excellent low-temperature resistance
KW  - Fiber-based wearable strain sensor
AB  - Fiber-based strain sensing materials, characterized by their small volume and high flexibility, can be woven into textiles or assembled into portable sensors adhered to the skin, making them ideal for wearable electronics. However, these materials, often constructed from conductive fillers and elastomers, frequently face challenges in providing high linear sensitivity and excellent low-temperature resistance due to the inherent limitations of their elastic conductive networks. Herein, a thermo/cryo-spun reduction strategy is proposed to fabricate a Hybrid Sensing Network Fiber (HSNF) consisting of ionic and electronic conductive networks. The HSNF comprises silver nanoparticle/polyvinyl alcohol/ethylene glycol (AgNPs/PVA/EG) conductive core gel encapsulated within a thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) sheath. This structure, benefiting from effective interface between the core and sheath, prevents core gel leakage. The hybrid sensing network enable the HSNF to exhibit high linear sensitivity (gauge factor of 2.26, linearity of 0.998) over a 100 % strain range. Furthermore, the inclusion of EG confers excellent low-temperature resistance, allowing the HSNF sensor to maintain stable operation at -20 degrees C. Conceptual demonstrations reveal that the HSNF sensor exhibits excellent electromechanical performance, making it suitable for monitoring human motion.
