KW  - alcohol
KW  - tobacco
AB  - Introduction. Alcohol and tobacco consumption remains a current public health problem in France. The training of future general practitioners (GPs) regarding addictions is disparate and educational objectives are not defined.
   Aim. To establish a list of functions expected of GPs and teaching items to be delivered to MG residents.
   Method. Elaboration of a consensus according to the Delphi procedure in 3 rounds on the basis of a review of recommendations and practice guidelines. 35 experts, GPs and addictologists, teachers and not, were recruited.
   Results. A significant consensus was reached. 32 expected functions and 41 teaching items were consensual. 10 key messages were identified to be conveyed. The majority of the expected functions were in line with those usually assigned to GPs. The results specified the limits of the expected GPs' skills. The development of a specific and consistent teaching in the 3(rd) cycle of medical studies was significant. Some clinical tools usually recommended to GPs were not selected as appropriate for primary healthcare.
   Conclusion. This work can be used for conception of specific teaching for residents or GPs in addictology.
