AB  - Many adolescents struggle with substance use and mental health concerns. Thus, identifying evidence-based prevention programming for youth in schools is of critical importance, both for reducing substance use and improving well-being. This study examines the efficacy of GPS for SUCCESS, a school-based substance use prevention program for adolescents, with a sample of n = 127 adolescents aged 16 to 18 years old in an alternative school setting. A paired-samples t-test revealed significant improvement for participating adolescents' personal growth initiative and confidence to abstain from substances, as measured by pre- and post-tests administered at the start and end of the five-week intervention. These findings may be useful for practitioners supporting youth in school settings. We provide implications for prevention programming, practice, and future research.
