TI  - Determinants of opiate use among fishermen-sailors
T2  - EXERCER-LA REVUE FRANCOPHONE DE MEDECINE GENERALE
LA  - French
AB  - Background. The consumption of opiates by sea fishermen is far above average. It appears in the 2007 report of the French Office for Drugs and Drug Addiction, and in other studies. One may wonder about the reasons for this excessive consumption.
   Objective. To analyse the relationship between being a fisherman and using heroin.
   Method. Qualitative study with semi-structured interviews of fishermen. Recruitment was done through their GPs. The interview grid consisted of a series of open questions about the job and addiction to opiates.
   Results. Seven fishermen were interviewed, aged from 23 to 56 years. They were known to be former heroin addicts, and treated with opiates replacement therapy by their GP. The sample followed the criteria of maximum variation. Saturation was achieved after the first five interviews. The most innovative items found were the concept of "no limit", prompting some fishermen to spend all the money earned in the toxic, the emotional loneliness, lack of companionship for assistance and exchange on board. Violence, bullying and often sexual hazing added the self-depreciation of abused persons to the stress of life at sea.
   Conclusion. Prevention campaigns on addiction among fishermen should focus on human respect to be more effective. These innovative items could be found in other socio-professional categories and lead to more targeted prevention campaigns.
